# Labels taken verbatim from the stock vendor_file_contexts, minus the entries
# for binaries this tree does not ship (vendor.lenovo.hardware.vibrator_dual has
# no service binary in the factory vendor image).

# Lenovo private AIDL HALs
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.battery-service     u:object_r:hal_lenovobattery_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.display-service     u:object_r:hal_lenovodisplay_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.hyperschedule-service u:object_r:hal_lenovohyperschedule_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.keyboard-service    u:object_r:hal_lenovokeyboard_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.misc-service        u:object_r:hal_lenovomisc_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.performance-service u:object_r:hal_lenovoperformance_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.touchscreen-service u:object_r:hal_lenovotouchscreen_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.lenovo\.hardware\.usb-service         u:object_r:hal_lenovousb_default_exec:s0

# Device nodes
/dev/ttyHS1                                                                 u:object_r:lenovo_keyboard_device:s0
/dev/lenovo_penraw                                                          u:object_r:lenovo_device:s0

# ── Vendor executables init service-starts that had no label ────────────────
# Found by work/scripts/36-sepolicy-labels.py, which recomputes labels against
# the built file_contexts rather than trusting that we wrote them. Every entry
# below is verbatim from the stock vendor_file_contexts unless noted.
#
# Without these, init cannot make the domain transition and the service runs as
# u:r:init:s0 (permissive) or not at all (enforcing). init's domain is broad
# enough that most of what they do is simply allowed, so a permissive boot
# produces no denials for them either — which is why these had to be written
# before the first flash rather than discovered from it.

# Third-party HALs — domains in hal_third_party.te
/(system/vendor|vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.microtrust\.hardware\.afw-service     u:object_r:hal_afw_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.dolby\.dms\.service                   u:object_r:hal_aidl_dms_default_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/dvs-aidl-service                              u:object_r:dvs_aidl_default_exec:s0
# The Dolby DAX Codec2 store reuses AOSP's mediacodec domain rather than getting
# one of its own — same as stock.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/vendor\.dolby\.media\.c2-default-service-dax  u:object_r:mediacodec_exec:s0

# Lenovo daemons — domains in daemons.te
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/prop_init                                        u:object_r:prop_init_exec:s0

# Adaptive backlight (CABL) and smart video intelligence (SVI) helper scripts.
# Stock applies this exact label to all five CABL and both SVI scripts. All seven
# just exec /vendor/bin/ppd with a different argument. The domain
# vendor_qti_init_shell already exists in device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr, so matching
# stock needs no device-specific .te rules — only these labels.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.cabl\.sh                             u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.cabl\.low\.sh                        u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.cabl\.medium\.sh                     u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.cabl\.high\.sh                       u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.cabl\.off\.sh                        u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.svi\.sh                              u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/init\.qcom\.svi\.off\.sh                         u:object_r:vendor_qti_init_shell_exec:s0

# Wi-Fi SAR / RF exposure limiting. vendor_wcnss_service already exists in the
# QTI vendor policy, so again label-only.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/wifisar\.sh                                      u:object_r:vendor_wcnss_service_exec:s0

# ⚠️ /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh is deliberately NOT labelled here, and it
# is worth writing down because "stock labels it, we don't" looks like a gap and is
# not one.
#
# Stock labels it tn_vendor_performance_shell_exec and its policy carries
#   typetransition init_202404 tn_vendor_performance_shell_exec process
#                  tn_vendor_performance_shell
# but that transition can never fire. The service line is
#   service tn_performance /vendor/bin/sh /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh
# so the file init actually execs is /vendor/bin/sh, and a domain transition keys on
# the type of the exec'd file, not on an argument. Measured on the live stock device:
#   ls -Z /vendor/bin/sh  ->  u:object_r:vendor_shell_exec:s0
#   ls -Z /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh -> u:object_r:tn_vendor_performance_shell_exec:s0
# The service therefore runs in whatever init transitions to for vendor_shell_exec on
# stock as well; Lenovo's dedicated domain is dead code.
#
# Copying the label would change nothing except read access to the script body, and
# porting the domain would be building a transition for an exec that does not happen.
# If the read-ahead tuning turns out to be denied on first boot, the fix is to give
# the *shell* domain the sysfs access, or to drop the .rc and .sh from
# proprietary-files.txt — not to invent a domain here.

# ── Runtime paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /data/vendor/afw is created from init by
# vendor.microtrust.hardware.afw-service.rc with no restorecon, so this label has
# to exist before first boot or the directory is created as vendor_data_file and
# stays that way.
/data/vendor/afw(/.*)?                                                       u:object_r:hal_afw_file:s0
/dev/afw                                                                     u:object_r:afw_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/proinfo                       u:object_r:vendor_proinfo_block_device:s0

# Awinic smart-amplifier calibration, read by hal_audio_default. Created and
# chowned from init.target.rc:162-167, never restorecon'd, so this has to be
# right before first boot. See sepolicy/vendor/hal_audio.te for the derivation.
/mnt/vendor/persist/factory/audio(/.*)?                                      u:object_r:vendor_persist_audio_file:s0

# ★ libsdmextension.so needs a SAME-PROCESS HAL label, and the label is right —
# but the MECHANISM this comment gave for eight sessions was wrong, and the wrong
# one is the more alarming of the two. Corrected session 37, by reading
# /proc/<pid>/maps rather than by re-reading the policy.
#
# It is QTI's display extension library and it really is mapped into a CORE
# process. What was inferred from that was `dlopen` from system_server, hence a
# `:file execute` that system/sepolicy/private/domain.te:1257-1271's
# full_treble_only neverallow FORBIDS for a coredomain over vendor_file_type.
# That story is false, and the maps say so in one column:
#
#   system_server  7322400000-7322635000 r--s  /vendor/lib64/libsdmextension.so
#   composer       773232d000-77323d0000 r--p  /vendor/lib64/libsdmextension.so
#                  77323d1000-7732554000 r-xp  …            <- a real dlopen
#                  7732555000-7732560000 r--p  …
#                  7732561000-7732562000 rw-p  …
#
# ONE read-only SHARED mapping in system_server, no executable segment. That is
# PinnerService's mmap(PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED) + mlock, and this port asks for it
# itself: the library is item 7 of config_defaultPinnerServiceFiles in
# overlay/FrameworkOverlayMalbec/res/values/config.xml:714. The linker is never
# involved, so `execute` is never requested and that neverallow never applied.
#
# ⚠️ Structurally it could not have been a dlopen either: readelf -d lists 14
# vendor DT_NEEDEDs (libsdmutils.so, libsdm-color.so, libdisplayqos.so,
# libsdedrm.so, …) and none of those is same_process_hal_file, so a real dlopen
# from system_server would have failed on the first dependency, not on this file.
#
# ★ The label is still REQUIRED, and the reason is smaller and checkable: there
# is NO `allow system_server vendor_file:file …` anywhere in the loaded policy
# (re-derived from the built plat_sepolicy.cil and vendor_sepolicy.cil: zero
# hits), so without it PinnerService's open/read/map is denied. With it,
# system/sepolicy/private/system_server.te:385 —
#     allow system_server same_process_hal_file:file
#           { execute read open getattr map };
# — already covers it, and no rule of ours is needed. The vendor side keeps
# working through domain.te:186.
#
# ★★ The honest justification is the SIBLING SYMMETRY, not the neverallow: the
# two OTHER vendor libraries in that same pinner array, libllvm-qgl.so and
# libllvm-glnext.so, are already same_process_hal_file upstream. libllvm-qgl.so
# is in fact BOTH pinned (r--s) and genuinely dlopen'd into system_server (r-xp)
# on this device — so the array mixes both cases and AOSP labels the ones it
# ships. This line finishes the set.
#
# ⚠️ Stock leaves it as plain `vendor_file` (its vendor_file_contexts has no line
# for it), so on the shipping ZUI image — which IS enforcing — system_server
# cannot pin this library. We are deliberately better than stock here rather than
# at parity.
/vendor/lib(64)?/libsdmextension\.so                                           u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
